Talk about a myth, you know.

522
00:24:41.000 --> 00:24:41.833
I love that.

523
00:24:41.833 --> 00:24:46.330
Well, this, you know, it always,

524
00:24:46.330 --> 00:24:47.560
you can look at someone's life

525
00:24:47.560 --> 00:24:48.950
through the rear view mirror and it's like,

526
00:24:48.950 --> 00:24:50.260
of course you're doing what you're doing.

527
00:24:50.260 --> 00:24:51.240
Like it all makes sense.

528
00:24:51.240 --> 00:24:52.880
All of these experiences that you've had

529
00:24:52.880 --> 00:24:57.180
have added up to you being the person that you are

530
00:24:57.180 --> 00:25:00.580
and the way that you kind of navigate the world.

531
00:25:00.580 --> 00:25:04.410
But looking back, it appears that this, you know,

532
00:25:04.410 --> 00:25:09.340
wanderlust and this sensitivity around poverty

533
00:25:09.340 --> 00:25:13.760
and being a caretaker for those who have less,

534
00:25:13.760 --> 00:25:17.063
has always been part and parcel of who you are.

535
00:25:18.600 --> 00:25:20.770
Yeah, I think talk about lucky.

536
00:25:20.770 --> 00:25:24.850
I think there are some people who don't figure out

537
00:25:24.850 --> 00:25:28.000
who they are or what they want to do

538
00:25:28.000 --> 00:25:31.350
at their most core part of who they are

539
00:25:31.350 --> 00:25:32.430
till very late in life.

540
00:25:32.430 --> 00:25:35.520
And I knew as a little girl

541
00:25:35.520 --> 00:25:38.950
that I was going to do something both to serve,

542
00:25:38.950 --> 00:25:43.220
and also as you said, to allow for a life of adventure

543
00:25:43.220 --> 00:25:45.230
and that's really carried itself through

544
00:25:47.160 --> 00:25:48.900
from the time I was six,

545
00:25:48.900 --> 00:25:52.270
but when I was probably 44 or 45,

546
00:25:52.270 --> 00:25:57.270
I got a random email from a boy I dated when I was 16.

547
00:25:57.450 --> 00:25:58.870
And he said I was in the dentist

548
00:25:58.870 --> 00:26:00.490
and I saw this magazine article.

549
00:26:00.490 --> 00:26:02.060
And I just wanna tell you Jacqueline,

550
00:26:02.060 --> 00:26:04.690
you're doing exactly today what you said you would be doing

551
00:26:04.690 --> 00:26:06.750
when you were 16 years old.

552
00:26:06.750 --> 00:26:08.750
And it really took my breath away

553
00:26:08.750 --> 00:26:12.810
because I don't remember being that clear as a 16 year old.

554
00:26:12.810 --> 00:26:16.473
I remember as a six year old wanting to save the world.

555
00:26:18.410 --> 00:26:23.250
I was a normal teenager but I think I had that sense

556
00:26:24.170 --> 00:26:27.853
that I wanted to make change.

557
00:26:28.810 --> 00:26:31.120
And certainly there's inflection points

558
00:26:31.120 --> 00:26:33.400
that end up pointing you in that direction,

559
00:26:33.400 --> 00:26:35.150
but I can't help but think,

560
00:26:35.150 --> 00:26:39.480
short of those stories that I want you to tell,

561
00:26:39.480 --> 00:26:42.210
that somehow you would've found your way to be doing exactly

562
00:26:42.210 --> 00:26:43.770
what you're doing anyway, right.

563
00:26:43.770 --> 00:26:45.020
Like by hook or crook.

564
00:26:45.020 --> 00:26:46.123
By hook or crook.

565
00:26:47.350 --> 00:26:50.160
So you end up going to UVA,

566
00:26:50.160 --> 00:26:53.500
you study like international relations and economics.

567
00:26:53.500 --> 00:26:55.200
But the idea was you wanted to be a writer.

568
00:26:55.200 --> 00:26:57.220
I mean, was the kind of, you know,

569
00:26:57.220 --> 00:27:00.183
war journalist thing, part of that idea at that time?

570
00:27:04.160 --> 00:27:06.580
Funny, I've never thought about that.

571
00:27:06.580 --> 00:27:10.833
In a way I think at Virginia,

572
00:27:12.270 --> 00:27:16.880
you know, my middle class army, military dad,

573
00:27:16.880 --> 00:27:19.870
immigrant dad was the one when he found out

574
00:27:19.870 --> 00:27:22.550
that I wanted to be an English major.

575
00:27:22.550 --> 00:27:25.520
In his very quiet but powerful way said

576
00:27:25.520 --> 00:27:27.570
that he thought that was a very bad idea.

577
00:27:28.460 --> 00:27:30.110
How would I ever support myself?

578
00:27:30.110 --> 00:27:33.703
And being a pleaser, certainly for him,

579
00:27:35.060 --> 00:27:40.060
at that time I took on economics,

580
00:27:40.610 --> 00:27:42.040
but I took every English course

581
00:27:42.040 --> 00:27:44.330
that I could get my hands on.

582
00:27:44.330 --> 00:27:49.260
And I think it was then, and I don't even know why,

583
00:27:49.260 --> 00:27:54.260
where I would read certain camus, and even Joseph Conrad,

584
00:27:56.490 --> 00:28:00.490
and find myself deeply drawn to this idea of good and evil

585
00:28:00.490 --> 00:28:03.280
and how are there good people and bad people,

586
00:28:03.280 --> 00:28:06.430
or do good and evil lurk within the spirits of every person,

587
00:28:06.430 --> 00:28:08.693
which is what I deeply believe now.

588
00:28:10.510 --> 00:28:14.963
And so maybe that was the connection then from early on,

589
00:28:15.907 --> 00:28:20.247
a pull toward conflict and broken places

590
00:28:24.730 --> 00:28:28.750
that started probably through my father being in Vietnam,

591
00:28:28.750 --> 00:28:30.150
but then through literature.

592
00:28:31.360 --> 00:28:34.680
So this desire to better understand the dynamics

593
00:28:34.680 --> 00:28:37.680
that would create that kind of conflict.

594
00:28:37.680 --> 00:28:39.490
Like are these people truly evil?

595
00:28:39.490 --> 00:28:42.280
Who's good and who's the bad guy?

596
00:28:42.280 --> 00:28:44.160
Or is there such a thing?

597
00:28:44.160 --> 00:28:47.109
I think it was, is there such a thing?

598
00:28:47.109 --> 00:28:48.942
Is there such a thing?

599
00:28:50.410 --> 00:28:52.630
That early on trying to understand

600
00:28:52.630 --> 00:28:54.180
the Contras and the Sandinistas

601
00:28:54.180 --> 00:28:57.473
which was part of my generation's moral conundrum.

602
00:28:59.230 --> 00:29:02.680
The deeper I would look into what was happening,

603
00:29:02.680 --> 00:29:07.283
the less sure I was of who was all good and who was all bad.

604
00:29:09.192 --> 00:29:12.430
And it was essentially recognizing

605
00:29:12.430 --> 00:29:15.440
that I didn't have such a black and white

606
00:29:15.440 --> 00:29:19.490
ability to come down with certainty that I thought

607
00:29:19.490 --> 00:29:21.773
I probably wouldn't be a very good journalist.

608
00:29:24.440 --> 00:29:26.320
But I wanted to know those places,

609
00:29:26.320 --> 00:29:28.865
I wanted to be in those places,

610
00:29:28.865 --> 00:29:31.040
I wanted to understand those places.

611
00:29:31.040 --> 00:29:33.380
And then layered on top of that,

612
00:29:33.380 --> 00:29:37.653
was this sensitivity around poverty,

613
00:29:39.350 --> 00:29:42.050
that comes in part from stories your dad would tell

614
00:29:42.050 --> 00:29:44.920
about being overseas being deployed

615
00:29:44.920 --> 00:29:47.883
and the poverty that he bore witness to.

616
00:29:49.110 --> 00:29:51.740
For sure, he's a quiet guy,

617
00:29:51.740 --> 00:29:54.990
but I would ask him questions all the time.

618
00:29:54.990 --> 00:29:59.740
And I was curious about the children mostly

619
00:29:59.740 --> 00:30:01.330
and my dad worked with an orphanage

620
00:30:01.330 --> 00:30:03.383
on one of his tours in Vietnam.

621
00:30:04.680 --> 00:30:06.240
And it didn't make sense to me

622
00:30:06.240 --> 00:30:10.713
that they were so poor and we were not.

623
00:30:12.070 --> 00:30:14.280
I would say, it was also very much,

624
00:30:14.280 --> 00:30:16.450
the Catholic school upbringing

625
00:30:16.450 --> 00:30:21.230
and this social justice element of my family.

626
00:30:21.230 --> 00:30:23.890
But honestly, Rich, it was also,

627
00:30:23.890 --> 00:30:26.530
sometimes I think you're born with parts,

628
00:30:26.530 --> 00:30:30.030
some of the seeds that become who you are,

629
00:30:30.030 --> 00:30:31.700
that as a little kid,

630
00:30:31.700 --> 00:30:36.700
I was always drawn to the runt of the puppy litter.

631
00:30:36.890 --> 00:30:39.170
You know, my mother tells the story of,

632
00:30:39.170 --> 00:30:41.210
you know, finally having enough money

633
00:30:41.210 --> 00:30:43.910
that they could to buy this perfect english sheepdog.

634
00:30:43.910 --> 00:30:47.040
I was probably nine and we went to the farm

635
00:30:47.040 --> 00:30:48.480
to get the perfect English sheep dog

636
00:30:48.480 --> 00:30:49.470
that she'd been studying.

637
00:30:49.470 --> 00:30:51.430
And then I saw the runt of the litter

638
00:30:51.430 --> 00:30:53.370
and was clearly had a problem

639
00:30:53.370 --> 00:30:55.910
and probably blind and mentally disabled.

640
00:30:55.910 --> 00:30:59.580
And I was like, how could you be such a horrible person

641
00:30:59.580 --> 00:31:01.660
that you're gonna make us get this perfect dog

642
00:31:01.660 --> 00:31:02.660
that every everybody will want,

643
00:31:02.660 --> 00:31:04.560
we need to take care of the little one?

644
00:31:04.560 --> 00:31:05.550
My mother was like, ohh.

645
00:31:05.550 --> 00:31:07.000
You're just wired that way.

646
00:31:07.910 --> 00:31:11.370
I think, I mean, I would ask you, do you think you're,

647
00:31:11.370 --> 00:31:15.370
like who you are is probably how you came out?

648
00:31:15.370 --> 00:31:18.690
A lot of it, you know, I believe in change as well.

649
00:31:18.690 --> 00:31:20.110
But I think constitutionally,

650
00:31:20.110 --> 00:31:21.660
yeah, we come out the way we are

651
00:31:21.660 --> 00:31:24.090
and, you know, as a parent of four kids, I can tell you,

652
00:31:24.090 --> 00:31:25.840
like-
which one?

653
00:31:25.840 --> 00:31:27.130
They come out pretty quickly.

654
00:31:27.130 --> 00:31:29.040
You can tell this is who this person is

655
00:31:29.040 --> 00:31:32.810
and they stay that way fundamentally throughout their lives.

656
00:31:32.810 --> 00:31:35.610
So, yeah, I think we come in with a certain wiring

657
00:31:35.610 --> 00:31:38.113
and inclinations and dispositions.

658
00:31:39.860 --> 00:31:42.930
And yet despite this, you end up, you know,

659
00:31:42.930 --> 00:31:44.720
in this interview for Goldman Sachs

660
00:31:44.720 --> 00:31:48.540
or not Goldman Sachs, Chase Manhattan, which is just like,

661
00:31:48.540 --> 00:31:50.193
you know, confusing.

662
00:31:52.460 --> 00:31:53.610
But I remember just to say,

663
00:31:53.610 --> 00:31:55.860
like, I'm a couple years younger than you,

664
00:31:55.860 --> 00:31:58.000
but I remember in college,

665
00:31:58.000 --> 00:32:01.490
the career centers and the career paths

666
00:32:01.490 --> 00:32:02.970
were not what they are today.

667
00:32:02.970 --> 00:32:04.480
Like you would there and they'd be like,

668
00:32:04.480 --> 00:32:06.720
well, it's Boston Consulting and it's Bain

669
00:32:06.720 --> 00:32:09.900
and it's this investment bank and that investment bank.

670
00:32:09.900 --> 00:32:11.920
And like that was kind of it.

671
00:32:11.920 --> 00:32:14.343
And that was even more advanced than when I went.

672
00:32:15.200 --> 00:32:19.600
You know, in our generation, in my generation,

673
00:32:19.600 --> 00:32:20.920
there weren't a lot of options.

674
00:32:20.920 --> 00:32:23.520
I didn't know what the nonprofit sector was.

675
00:32:23.520 --> 00:32:25.200
I knew that there's charities,

676
00:32:25.200 --> 00:32:28.050
but I didn't think there was an interesting career there.

677
00:32:29.390 --> 00:32:32.170
I thought that if you work for them, you didn't get paid.

678
00:32:32.170 --> 00:32:34.400
The only way to do it would be to volunteer.

679
00:32:34.400 --> 00:32:36.750
And you were sort of Saint Lee, yeah, me too.

680
00:32:38.477 --> 00:32:40.830
And I had paid for college.

681
00:32:40.830 --> 00:32:44.700
I really wasn't that interested in going straight into work

682
00:32:44.700 --> 00:32:47.700
after I had been working nonstop for my whole life.

683
00:32:47.700 --> 00:32:50.890
I wanted to go, you know, ski in Colorado for a year

684
00:32:50.890 --> 00:32:55.010
and experience what all my friends had had in their lives

685
00:32:55.010 --> 00:32:57.930
that I'd never really been able to do, fun.

686
00:32:57.930 --> 00:32:59.853
In that way, I always had fun.

687
00:33:02.890 --> 00:33:07.890
And so our career office just had these little boxes

688
00:33:08.430 --> 00:33:10.780
where you could put your resume into the different boxes

689
00:33:10.780 --> 00:33:14.170
that took your majors.

690
00:33:14.170 --> 00:33:16.760
And so I put my name in the boxes

691
00:33:16.760 --> 00:33:18.450
and Chase Manhattan called

692
00:33:18.450 --> 00:33:20.690
and I really said to my roommates.

693
00:33:20.690 --> 00:33:22.630
I was living with these two women, I was like,

694
00:33:22.630 --> 00:33:25.150
this is ridiculous, I don't wanna do this interview.

695
00:33:25.150 --> 00:33:26.280
And they're like, just do it.

696
00:33:26.280 --> 00:33:29.520
I promised my mom and dad that I would do the interviews

697
00:33:29.520 --> 00:33:32.310
and I had one suit that I owned.

698
00:33:32.310 --> 00:33:34.810
And so I walk into this interview

699
00:33:34.810 --> 00:33:36.930
with my foreign affairs and economics degree.

700
00:33:36.930 --> 00:33:39.400
And this really cute guy is sitting at a table

701
00:33:39.400 --> 00:33:40.233
in this little room.

702
00:33:40.233 --> 00:33:42.550
And he says, "So Ms Novogratz,

703
00:33:42.550 --> 00:33:44.500
why do you wanna be a banker?"

704
00:33:44.500 --> 00:33:46.240
And it was truly the one question

705
00:33:46.240 --> 00:33:50.410
I was fully unprepared to answer, not being a liar.

706
00:33:50.410 --> 00:33:52.350
I just didn't hesitate.

707
00:33:52.350 --> 00:33:54.260
And I said, I don't wanna be a banker.

708
00:33:54.260 --> 00:33:56.610
My mom and dad are making me do this interview.

709
00:33:57.961 --> 00:34:00.853
And you could just see his face, just like,

710
00:34:02.180 --> 00:34:04.350
huh, well, that's really unfortunate

711
00:34:04.350 --> 00:34:05.640
because if you got this job,

712
00:34:05.640 --> 00:34:08.530
you would be in 40 countries in the next three years,

713
00:34:08.530 --> 00:34:12.987
learning all about the political and economic structures

714
00:34:12.987 --> 00:34:15.530
and situations in those countries.

715
00:34:15.530 --> 00:34:18.620
Which of course was a big part of really what I wanted to do

716
00:34:18.620 --> 00:34:20.260
with my life, was to go-

717
00:34:20.260 --> 00:34:22.450
I had never been outside the United States,

718
00:34:22.450 --> 00:34:24.083
barely on a plane at that point.

719
00:34:25.909 --> 00:34:27.970
And so then it was just like, man.

720
00:34:27.970 --> 00:34:29.630
So I said, "Do you think we could

721
00:34:30.520 --> 00:34:32.420
start this interview over?"

722
00:34:32.420 --> 00:34:35.080
And he smiled and said, "Sure."

723
00:34:35.080 --> 00:34:38.660
So got up, walked out of the room, knocked at the door,

724
00:34:38.660 --> 00:34:40.640
extended my hand, said, you know,

725
00:34:40.640 --> 00:34:41.890
hi, I'm Jacqueline Novogratz.

726
00:34:41.890 --> 00:34:43.840
And he said, "So tell me, Miss Novogratz,

727
00:34:43.840 --> 00:34:45.280
why do you wanna be a banker?"

728
00:34:45.280 --> 00:34:48.810
And I said, ever since I was five years old,

729
00:34:48.810 --> 00:34:50.810
all I've ever wanted to be was a banker.

730
00:34:52.183 --> 00:34:54.420
And he laughed, I laughed.

731
00:34:54.420 --> 00:34:57.660
And he is like, so, you know, let's see how good you are,

732
00:34:57.660 --> 00:35:00.240
that he would throw out these questions that he'd be like,

733
00:35:00.240 --> 00:35:03.230
so you're foreign affairs and your real focus

734
00:35:03.230 --> 00:35:06.630
is Eastern Europe and Southeast Asia.

735
00:35:06.630 --> 00:35:08.290
So tell me about the Middle East.

736
00:35:08.290 --> 00:35:10.170
And I'd say, I don't know anything about the Middle East.

737
00:35:10.170 --> 00:35:13.650
He's like, "I know, tell me about it anyway."

738
00:35:13.650 --> 00:35:15.300
I was like, okay.

739
00:35:15.300 --> 00:35:17.770
You're like, this is going very well.

740
00:35:17.770 --> 00:35:21.110
I just assumed there is no way on earth

741
00:35:21.110 --> 00:35:23.160
that this guy is gonna move me to the next level.

742
00:35:23.160 --> 00:35:24.990
And I was a bartender.

743
00:35:24.990 --> 00:35:28.230
I went to the bar, worked till three in the morning.

744
00:35:28.230 --> 00:35:30.990
My little suit was crumpled into a ball

745
00:35:30.990 --> 00:35:34.270
in the back of my backpack that I rode on my bike home.

746
00:35:34.270 --> 00:35:38.210
Get home and pre-cell phones on the little table by the door

747
00:35:38.210 --> 00:35:40.447
is a piece of paper that says,

748
00:35:40.447 --> 00:35:42.570
"Chase called you back 7:00 AM interviewing."

749
00:35:42.570 --> 00:35:46.854
I'm thinking, okay, how are we gonna pull this one off?

750
00:35:46.854 --> 00:35:48.838
You're gonna have to iron that suit.

751
00:35:48.838 --> 00:35:51.280
Got the iron, got the blow dryer.

752
00:35:51.280 --> 00:35:53.210
I mean, Jacqueline, it's so crazy.

753
00:35:53.210 --> 00:35:54.680
And I know you've been asked this before,

754
00:35:54.680 --> 00:35:57.720
but there's a couple interesting things about this.

755
00:35:57.720 --> 00:36:01.230
I mean, not the least of which is the audacity

756
00:36:01.230 --> 00:36:05.520
or the gumption to tell the guy you don't want the job

757
00:36:05.520 --> 00:36:07.010
right out of the gate.

758
00:36:07.010 --> 00:36:08.090
That's very unusual.

759
00:36:08.090 --> 00:36:11.000
Most people, you know, would be a shrinking flower

760
00:36:11.000 --> 00:36:12.640
in the wake of such a question

761
00:36:12.640 --> 00:36:17.170
and try to scramble to answer it in an appeasing way,

762
00:36:17.170 --> 00:36:18.170
I suppose.

763
00:36:18.170 --> 00:36:20.900
And then the second piece of asking for a do-over.

764
00:36:20.900 --> 00:36:24.140
So there's this kind of truth telling compulsion

765
00:36:24.140 --> 00:36:27.640
that you have and also this fearlessness

766
00:36:27.640 --> 00:36:29.480
to kind of ask the question

767
00:36:29.480 --> 00:36:31.773
that most of us would be too afraid to ask.

768
00:36:37.588 --> 00:36:40.379
And on the point of you being asked this question before,

769
00:36:40.379 --> 00:36:43.980
I still don't know that I've gotten a satisfactory answer.

770
00:36:43.980 --> 00:36:47.960
Yeah, yeah, yeah.
On where that comes from.

771
00:36:51.810 --> 00:36:56.120
I do think inside of me and maybe everybody is this,

772
00:36:56.120 --> 00:37:01.120
both the cocky and the unsure,

773
00:37:01.760 --> 00:37:03.293
the audacious and the humble.

774
00:37:04.650 --> 00:37:08.243
The worthy and the not sure how worthy you actually are.

775
00:37:11.234 --> 00:37:15.300
And I do know that I am a truth teller

776
00:37:15.300 --> 00:37:19.760
so much so that I'm really, I can't read speeches.

777
00:37:19.760 --> 00:37:24.340
You know, I have to connect and just speak

778
00:37:24.340 --> 00:37:27.183
and it drives my communications team insane.

779
00:37:28.654 --> 00:37:30.480
And that's related to it somehow,

780
00:37:30.480 --> 00:37:33.903
that if I know what I'm talking about,

781
00:37:35.367 --> 00:37:36.930
I can talk about it,

782
00:37:36.930 --> 00:37:40.730
but I can't make something up

783
00:37:40.730 --> 00:37:42.380
unless everyone knows I'm making it up

784
00:37:42.380 --> 00:37:44.260
and then I can have fun.

785
00:37:44.260 --> 00:37:48.290
So I think the first part was, it was the truth

786
00:37:49.200 --> 00:37:53.510
and I wasn't looking for a job and I was only doing it

787
00:37:53.510 --> 00:37:55.730
because my mom and dad made me do it.

788
00:37:55.730 --> 00:37:57.440
Not made me do it, but, you know.

789
00:37:57.440 --> 00:38:00.180
And if you didn't want it, there wasn't much to lose.

790
00:38:00.180 --> 00:38:01.480
The stakes weren't high.

791
00:38:02.430 --> 00:38:05.510
It felt so ridiculous to me that I was in there

792
00:38:05.510 --> 00:38:08.743
in this little gray suit that I got it hit or miss,

793
00:38:10.270 --> 00:38:13.670
trying to maybe get this job, or maybe not,

794
00:38:13.670 --> 00:38:14.773
I didn't even know.

795
00:38:17.265 --> 00:38:20.670
And then the idea for me at that point

796
00:38:22.660 --> 00:38:26.393
of traveling to 40 countries was like,

797
00:38:27.560 --> 00:38:30.763
like not a possibility at some level.

798
00:38:31.600 --> 00:38:33.980
And again, you know, I'm sure in my mind there was like,

799
00:38:33.980 --> 00:38:35.582
I will travel the world.

800
00:38:35.582 --> 00:38:38.097
Like Gertrude Bell on a camel, right.

801
00:38:38.097 --> 00:38:41.930
You know, go off and be the lone wander,

802
00:38:41.930 --> 00:38:46.359
not someone saying on a plate, I'm gonna hand this to,

803
00:38:46.359 --> 00:38:47.750
you want it?

804
00:38:47.750 --> 00:38:50.954
And there was no way I was gonna say no.

805
00:38:50.954 --> 00:38:55.954
Yeah, it's exactly what you were hoping to manifest

806
00:38:57.040 --> 00:38:57.873
in your life.

807
00:38:57.873 --> 00:39:01.710
And you would then be getting paid to travel

808
00:39:01.710 --> 00:39:04.180
all of these places and immerse yourself

809
00:39:04.180 --> 00:39:07.470
in the economies of all of these foreign countries

810
00:39:07.470 --> 00:39:09.980
and garner this unbelievable education

811
00:39:09.980 --> 00:39:11.670
about how the world works.

812
00:39:11.670 --> 00:39:15.080
And in truth, I had no idea what banking even was anyway.

813
00:39:15.080 --> 00:39:18.810
All I saw was 40 countries where I cared about politics.

814
00:39:18.810 --> 00:39:20.730
I cared about the economies.

815
00:39:20.730 --> 00:39:22.333
I cared about people.

816
00:39:22.333 --> 00:39:24.570
I didn't really know what it meant to be a banker.

817
00:39:24.570 --> 00:39:28.230
And in fact, it was funny when I first came back to the bar

818
00:39:28.230 --> 00:39:30.280
I worked at in the summertime

819
00:39:30.280 --> 00:39:35.250
and everybody was so proud of me for being a bank teller,

820
00:39:35.250 --> 00:39:38.963
which was sort of what everyone thought a banker was.

821
00:39:40.270 --> 00:39:41.810
And truth was I made more money at the bar

822
00:39:41.810 --> 00:39:42.950
than I did at the bank anyway.

823
00:39:42.950 --> 00:39:46.593
So it was a funny moment.

824
00:39:47.600 --> 00:39:50.720
The idea of investment banking and consulting,

825
00:39:50.720 --> 00:39:52.593
I was still a few years away I think.

826
00:39:53.720 --> 00:39:57.280
So you get the job, you move to New York,

827
00:39:57.280 --> 00:39:59.500
and suddenly you're on a plane and you're flying around

828
00:39:59.500 --> 00:40:01.010
and like, what are you actually doing

829
00:40:01.010 --> 00:40:02.710
when you're going to these places?

830
00:40:05.210 --> 00:40:09.540
So it was called credit audit

831
00:40:09.540 --> 00:40:13.580
and we would be essentially handed this big stack of loans

832
00:40:13.580 --> 00:40:17.190
that Hong Kong or Kuala Lumpur

833
00:40:17.190 --> 00:40:20.720
or Buenos Aires offices had made.

834
00:40:20.720 --> 00:40:24.880
And we would go on these big yellow sheets of paper.

835
00:40:24.880 --> 00:40:27.660
We would look at the financials.

836
00:40:27.660 --> 00:40:29.170
We'd look at the purpose of the loan.

837
00:40:29.170 --> 00:40:32.410
We'd look at how it was structured.

838
00:40:32.410 --> 00:40:36.410
Was it variable interest or long term interest?

839
00:40:36.410 --> 00:40:41.410
And we would assess the credit worthiness

840
00:40:41.530 --> 00:40:44.730
of the bank's loan portfolio.

841
00:40:44.730 --> 00:40:47.950
The early '80s was a really powerful time to do that

842
00:40:47.950 --> 00:40:52.950
because you had wild inflation and wild financial crisis.

843
00:40:56.560 --> 00:40:58.580
So you actually had hyperinflation.

844
00:40:58.580 --> 00:41:03.580
In one day, Brazil's currency could change by 800%,

845
00:41:04.720 --> 00:41:06.720
always in the wrong way for Brazil.

846
00:41:06.720 --> 00:41:10.460
So suddenly, company after company and nation after nation

847
00:41:10.460 --> 00:41:11.910
was defaulting on their debt.

848
00:41:12.920 --> 00:41:15.640
The same time, the Saudi gold,

849
00:41:15.640 --> 00:41:18.190
I mean, oil was becoming like gold.

850
00:41:18.190 --> 00:41:20.580
And so the banks were really liquid

851
00:41:20.580 --> 00:41:21.980
or had been really liquid.

852
00:41:21.980 --> 00:41:23.980
And so they were making stupider and stupider loans

853
00:41:23.980 --> 00:41:26.120
because they had this money to push out the door.

854
00:41:26.120 --> 00:41:29.050
And so from a macro perspective,

855
00:41:29.050 --> 00:41:31.240
I found it just fascinating,

856
00:41:31.240 --> 00:41:32.630
because for the first time in my life,

857
00:41:32.630 --> 00:41:35.780
I saw the world as all connected.

858
00:41:35.780 --> 00:41:40.440
That was happening in oil had a direct influence

859
00:41:40.440 --> 00:41:42.620
on who was getting money

860
00:41:42.620 --> 00:41:45.540
in another completely different part of the world.

861
00:41:45.540 --> 00:41:47.700
I also saw how the rich were treated so differently

862
00:41:47.700 --> 00:41:49.080
from the poor.

863
00:41:49.080 --> 00:41:51.930
And that a hundred million dollar loans

864
00:41:51.930 --> 00:41:56.150
would be made to elites in, I keep picking on Brazil,

865
00:41:56.150 --> 00:42:00.510
but that was the country that had such an impact on me.

866
00:42:00.510 --> 00:42:04.010
And it was clear if you,

867
00:42:04.010 --> 00:42:09.010
once I learned to see story and narrative in financials,

868
00:42:10.030 --> 00:42:13.070
it was clear that the person who borrowed

869
00:42:13.070 --> 00:42:17.180
had no intention to ever repay.

870
00:42:17.180 --> 00:42:19.100
Sometimes they would move the money right offshore

871
00:42:19.100 --> 00:42:22.590
to protect it so that they had the money.

872
00:42:22.590 --> 00:42:25.633
Sometimes they would use part of it.

873
00:42:27.550 --> 00:42:29.240
But there was a lot of fraud

874
00:42:29.240 --> 00:42:32.410
and the bankers often didn't follow up.

875
00:42:32.410 --> 00:42:36.090
Everything was based on a handshaking relationship

876
00:42:36.090 --> 00:42:39.493
based on being in the same club.

877
00:42:40.720 --> 00:42:45.720
And so I found that both fascinating and terrifying,

878
00:42:47.020 --> 00:42:48.683
and at the same time,

879
00:42:51.480 --> 00:42:54.210
because we all read about the developing world

880
00:42:54.210 --> 00:42:55.960
is these really poor places.

881
00:42:55.960 --> 00:43:00.930
And yet there I was in this incredibly alive, beautiful,

882
00:43:00.930 --> 00:43:04.210
colorful, joyous place.

883
00:43:04.210 --> 00:43:09.210
I would be drawn into communities that were poor.

884
00:43:09.810 --> 00:43:10.980
I didn't think of them as poor.

885
00:43:10.980 --> 00:43:13.569
I just saw them as the most colorful, vibrant,

886
00:43:13.569 --> 00:43:15.020
joyful, communities.

887
00:43:15.020 --> 00:43:19.180
And then I realized those people were fully excluded.

888
00:43:19.180 --> 00:43:24.130
They would never have a chance to get even a $10 loan

889
00:43:24.130 --> 00:43:26.570
and maybe they would be better at repaying it,

890
00:43:26.570 --> 00:43:30.440
because they would have such a sense of obligation

891
00:43:30.440 --> 00:43:34.780
in ways that the too many, obviously not everyone,

892
00:43:34.780 --> 00:43:38.320
but too many of the elites weren't taking seriously.

893
00:43:38.320 --> 00:43:41.740
So walk me through the process of how you

894
00:43:41.740 --> 00:43:43.900
take those experiences.

895
00:43:43.900 --> 00:43:47.460
The more that you kind of understand the tectonic plates

896
00:43:47.460 --> 00:43:50.610
of macroeconomics and how it's sort of marginalizing

897
00:43:50.610 --> 00:43:54.510
and depriving these poor people of access to capital

898
00:43:54.510 --> 00:43:56.080
and getting to understand

899
00:43:56.080 --> 00:43:59.140
kind of what that dynamic looks like,

900
00:43:59.140 --> 00:44:04.140
how you step into this idea of possibly tackling it,

901
00:44:07.000 --> 00:44:08.990
you know, as a vocation.

902
00:44:08.990 --> 00:44:10.950
You're a banker, I mean, you could have stayed there

903
00:44:10.950 --> 00:44:13.270
and had this successful career.

904
00:44:13.270 --> 00:44:14.940
You were moving up the ladder,

905
00:44:14.940 --> 00:44:18.390
but at some point you jump off the chain.

906
00:44:18.390 --> 00:44:21.563
You were talking about stakes in that first interview.

907
00:44:22.740 --> 00:44:23.810
High stakes, low stakes.

908
00:44:23.810 --> 00:44:27.430
I think it plays a big role in human beings lives

909
00:44:27.430 --> 00:44:29.973
in those decision points.

910
00:44:31.220 --> 00:44:32.460
Are you in a high stakes moment?

911
00:44:32.460 --> 00:44:33.970
Are you in a low stakes moment?

912
00:44:33.970 --> 00:44:36.220
And sometimes when it's a high stakes moment,

913
00:44:37.170 --> 00:44:40.950
there's greater clarity to what the decision actually means.

914
00:44:40.950 --> 00:44:45.030
So I would say two, if not three things,

915
00:44:45.030 --> 00:44:46.520
were happening simultaneously.

916
00:44:46.520 --> 00:44:51.520
One, I'd had this epiphany that the poor had no access.

917
00:44:54.410 --> 00:44:55.720
And yet we were writing off

918
00:44:55.720 --> 00:44:57.770
hundreds of millions of dollars of loans.

919
00:45:00.692 --> 00:45:02.730
I didn't think I needed to leave the bank

920
00:45:02.730 --> 00:45:05.420
because I actually loved so much about it

921
00:45:05.420 --> 00:45:09.383
and some of my very best friends, I met in that period.

922
00:45:11.890 --> 00:45:16.890
And I thought that my boss might be interested in the idea

923
00:45:17.060 --> 00:45:20.680
of me building a unit inside of the bank

924
00:45:20.680 --> 00:45:23.320
where we actually lent to low income people,

925
00:45:23.320 --> 00:45:27.150
because I was sure we were gonna get better repayment rates

926
00:45:27.150 --> 00:45:32.150
than the bank was getting at a macro level.

927
00:45:33.860 --> 00:45:36.480
The second thing that happened,

928
00:45:36.480 --> 00:45:37.820
I guess there were three things.

929
00:45:37.820 --> 00:45:41.200
At the time, I stumbled across this article about

930
00:45:41.200 --> 00:45:43.930
a little known man at the time named Muhammad Yunus,

931
00:45:43.930 --> 00:45:46.320
who ended up being the founder of the Grameen Bank

932
00:45:46.320 --> 00:45:48.340
and got the Nobel Peace Prize

933
00:45:48.340 --> 00:45:51.143
for the work he did on just this idea.

934
00:45:52.371 --> 00:45:54.440
He was the pioneer of microfinance.

935
00:45:54.440 --> 00:45:56.960
He was one of the pioneer,

936
00:45:56.960 --> 00:45:59.120
but he was the greatest storyteller.

937
00:45:59.120 --> 00:46:04.120
And I would say there were three or four, but it was 1976.

938
00:46:05.120 --> 00:46:06.700
Talk about patience,

939
00:46:06.700 --> 00:46:09.590
when he first made 30 small loans

940
00:46:09.590 --> 00:46:12.200
to 30 desperately poor Bangladeshi women

941
00:46:12.200 --> 00:46:14.110
and saw that they all paid back

942
00:46:14.110 --> 00:46:16.750
in a nation where the average were payment rates

943
00:46:16.750 --> 00:46:21.060
at the banks were something like 15%, 85% write-offs, right.

944
00:46:21.060 --> 00:46:25.160
So I read that, I was seeing it with my own eyes

945
00:46:25.160 --> 00:46:26.280
and went to the bank.

946
00:46:26.280 --> 00:46:27.630
And then the third thing that happened

947
00:46:27.630 --> 00:46:30.550
was this incredible man, Tony Terracciano,

948
00:46:30.550 --> 00:46:33.593
who was the number two person at Chase,

949
00:46:34.480 --> 00:46:39.480
had offered me the chance to essentially

950
00:46:39.800 --> 00:46:44.800
sit at his right side and build a fast track career.

951
00:46:47.020 --> 00:46:48.680
And he was an extraordinary man.

952
00:46:48.680 --> 00:46:53.680
I think he saw in me this scrappy person that he was and is

953
00:46:57.500 --> 00:47:01.070
and so he kept raising the stakes, you know.

954
00:47:01.070 --> 00:47:02.860
I wasn't fighting anything that was perfect.

955
00:47:02.860 --> 00:47:04.820
I couldn't find anything in Brazil.

956
00:47:04.820 --> 00:47:09.393
I knew I had stumbled upon this and I wanted to do it,

957
00:47:10.740 --> 00:47:14.823
somehow test whether you could lend to low income people.

958
00:47:18.800 --> 00:47:20.980
And so I was stumbling or fumbling

959
00:47:20.980 --> 00:47:23.040
toward finding an opportunity.

960
00:47:23.040 --> 00:47:25.730
Mr. Terracciano kept offering,

961
00:47:25.730 --> 00:47:27.210
well, you wanna work in Spain?

962
00:47:27.210 --> 00:47:28.250
Where do you wanna work?

963
00:47:28.250 --> 00:47:29.113
Pick a place.

964
00:47:30.750 --> 00:47:33.450
The Texas banks were hurting.

965
00:47:33.450 --> 00:47:36.053
Go fix that, go help me fix that problem.

966
00:47:38.934 --> 00:47:41.980
And I think that was the clarifying moment for me,

967
00:47:41.980 --> 00:47:45.363
that here's a fast track, here's no track.

968
00:47:47.840 --> 00:47:49.710
I don't know if you'll ever get on that fast track,

969
00:47:49.710 --> 00:47:52.750
and my father definitely thought that I had thrown away,

970
00:47:52.750 --> 00:47:55.300
you know, that once in a lifetime opportunity

971
00:47:55.300 --> 00:48:00.300
and I might have, but I knew that if I went with him,

972
00:48:01.880 --> 00:48:03.210
I would've stayed on that track.

973
00:48:03.210 --> 00:48:05.920
That's a really hard track to get off of.

974
00:48:05.920 --> 00:48:08.740
And there was no opportunity for you to say to him,

975
00:48:08.740 --> 00:48:13.740
let's start a microfinance branch in Brazil

976
00:48:14.400 --> 00:48:16.650
and see how it goes and let me run it

977
00:48:16.650 --> 00:48:18.750
and prove to you that we can make it work?

978
00:48:20.260 --> 00:48:22.730
Or was that something that was just too far afield

979
00:48:22.730 --> 00:48:25.430
and not interesting to-
It's such a great question.

980
00:48:29.134 --> 00:48:32.970
I might have lacked the courage or the imagination

981
00:48:32.970 --> 00:48:37.830
because I asked my boss, who I didn't respect as much,

982
00:48:37.830 --> 00:48:40.460
but maybe because this was like,

983
00:48:40.460 --> 00:48:41.760
it was kind of,

984
00:48:41.760 --> 00:48:43.800
remember that show get smart with the doors

985
00:48:43.800 --> 00:48:45.569
that opened and shut?
Yeah, this is the big deal.

986
00:48:45.569 --> 00:48:47.310
To get to the 60th floor,
You're in the executive

987
00:48:47.310 --> 00:48:48.156
washroom going-
You took this big

988
00:48:48.156 --> 00:48:50.247
all the way to the top and the doors would open

989
00:48:50.247 --> 00:48:53.140
and he would be sitting behind this desk and I was there

990
00:48:54.194 --> 00:48:55.027
with my, you know-

991
00:48:55.027 --> 00:48:56.260
And you're 25, right, or something like that.

992
00:48:56.260 --> 00:48:57.553
And I'm 25 years old.

993
00:49:01.600 --> 00:49:05.030
Yeah, I think I may have lacked the courage

994
00:49:06.320 --> 00:49:07.660
to do a deal with them.

995
00:49:07.660 --> 00:49:10.593
It was either the deal's on the table,

996
00:49:11.800 --> 00:49:14.827
or go off into this unknown territory.

997
00:49:14.827 --> 00:49:16.860
So you split?

998
00:49:16.860 --> 00:49:17.693
So I split.

999
00:49:17.693 --> 00:49:19.783
Yeah, how were mom and dad with that?

1000
00:49:21.580 --> 00:49:22.413
Not great.

1001
00:49:24.060 --> 00:49:26.920
And again, you know, context in time,

1002
00:49:26.920 --> 00:49:29.640
I was going to a place they'd never been able to imagine.

1003
00:49:29.640 --> 00:49:30.903
Very few of us could.

1004
00:49:32.240 --> 00:49:35.220
No cell phones, no real way of connecting.

1005
00:49:35.220 --> 00:49:36.770
I didn't have health insurance.

1006
00:49:37.690 --> 00:49:40.440
I couldn't explain to anybody exactly what I was doing.

1007
00:49:44.240 --> 00:49:46.870
And ma'am, was it easy for them to tell people

1008
00:49:46.870 --> 00:49:49.330
that their daughter, and there weren't that many young women

1009
00:49:49.330 --> 00:49:51.350
who worked on Wall Street at the time,

1010
00:49:51.350 --> 00:49:52.960
had this fancy job where she was traveling

1011
00:49:52.960 --> 00:49:54.900
all around the world telling bankers

1012
00:49:54.900 --> 00:49:57.550
how to do their business better, that's a good story.

1013
00:49:59.290 --> 00:50:02.050
Telling them that they were living in,

1014
00:50:02.050 --> 00:50:04.500
I was living in places that they couldn't find out a map,

1015
00:50:04.500 --> 00:50:06.340
doing something that they didn't understand

1016
00:50:06.340 --> 00:50:07.840
or weren't able to articulate.

1017
00:50:09.970 --> 00:50:12.270
Didn't feel quite the same.

1018
00:50:12.270 --> 00:50:13.780
And then there was the fear factor,

1019
00:50:13.780 --> 00:50:15.410
I think, particularly for my mother.

1020
00:50:15.410 --> 00:50:16.963
My father had been in Vietnam.

1021
00:50:18.280 --> 00:50:22.930
For my mother, I was never gonna get married.

1022
00:50:22.930 --> 00:50:25.110
That was a big piece for her.

1023
00:50:25.110 --> 00:50:30.110
And something bad was surely going to happen to me.

1024
00:50:31.360 --> 00:50:34.403
And whatever come back.

1025
00:50:35.950 --> 00:50:37.430
I think we under-appreciate

1026
00:50:37.430 --> 00:50:40.810
the extent to which those social pressures

1027
00:50:40.810 --> 00:50:43.313
keep us in situations we don't wanna be in.

1028
00:50:44.210 --> 00:50:47.130
It takes a lot of courage when-

1029
00:50:47.130 --> 00:50:48.640
You're the oldest, right?
Mmm-hmm.

1030
00:50:48.640 --> 00:50:49.910
of all your siblings?

1031
00:50:49.910 --> 00:50:53.410
I'm sure your parents were very invested in your path

1032
00:50:53.410 --> 00:50:56.193
and all of that and it's hard.

1033
00:50:57.560 --> 00:50:59.560
Well, and also of maybe invested in me

1034
00:51:00.730 --> 00:51:03.350
continuing to be a role model and a helper

1035
00:51:03.350 --> 00:51:06.053
in the six that were coming up.

1036
00:51:07.400 --> 00:51:10.880
So, yeah, and you're a parent.

1037
00:51:10.880 --> 00:51:12.470
You wanna keep your children safe?

1038
00:51:12.470 --> 00:51:14.320
Sure, of course.

1039
00:51:14.320 --> 00:51:16.823
So how do you figure out what the next move is?

1040
00:51:21.760 --> 00:51:23.490
There was a woman at,

1041
00:51:23.490 --> 00:51:27.403
well, I wrote to the big names:

1042
00:51:29.280 --> 00:51:33.663
Muhammad Yunus, Abed, Ilabat in India.

1043
00:51:35.930 --> 00:51:37.640
Those were the days where it took 10 days

1044
00:51:37.640 --> 00:51:40.320
to get a letter across the world.

1045
00:51:40.320 --> 00:51:41.870
Nobody wrote me back,

1046
00:51:41.870 --> 00:51:45.490
but a woman I worked with had an aunt named Michaela Walsh

1047
00:51:45.490 --> 00:51:47.140
who worked with Women's World Banking

1048
00:51:47.140 --> 00:51:49.570
and she was in New York city.

1049
00:51:49.570 --> 00:51:54.570
And so I went to her and I said I wanted to go to Brazil

1050
00:51:54.747 --> 00:51:57.140
and I would help build Women's World Banking in Brazil.

1051
00:51:57.140 --> 00:51:58.850
And she said, that was great,

1052
00:51:58.850 --> 00:52:00.260
but they had no opportunities there.

1053
00:52:00.260 --> 00:52:03.100
And there was, however, a job in West Africa

1054
00:52:03.100 --> 00:52:04.780
in the Cote d'Ivoire.

1055
00:52:04.780 --> 00:52:09.780
Not exactly on my game plan, but I said, yes.

1056
00:52:11.380 --> 00:52:12.840
Didn't ask for any details.

1057
00:52:12.840 --> 00:52:14.540
Missed the health insurance piece.

1058
00:52:18.560 --> 00:52:23.560
And literally, probably a month later, sold everything.

1059
00:52:23.600 --> 00:52:25.130
My mother still reminds me,

1060
00:52:25.130 --> 00:52:26.650
you know, she had given me some good antiques

1061
00:52:26.650 --> 00:52:28.160
that were just left on the corner

1062
00:52:28.160 --> 00:52:29.677
first subway to pick up and-

1063
00:52:31.050 --> 00:52:32.280
Cote d'Ivoire

1064
00:52:32.280 --> 00:52:33.993
There I was, Cote d'Ivoire.

1065
00:52:34.870 --> 00:52:36.890
So I feel like this is another,

1066
00:52:36.890 --> 00:52:39.720
you know, this experience is another inflection point

1067
00:52:39.720 --> 00:52:43.050
in your education of appreciating the complexity

1068
00:52:43.050 --> 00:52:45.360
and nuance of the problems that you were,

1069
00:52:45.360 --> 00:52:47.543
you know, audacious enough to even tackle.

1070
00:52:48.880 --> 00:52:50.343
On so many levels, Rich.

1071
00:52:54.900 --> 00:52:57.173
Audaciousness but also arrogance.

1072
00:52:58.130 --> 00:53:01.480
It never dawned on me to ask

1073
00:53:01.480 --> 00:53:04.223
whether I would be properly introduced.

1074
00:53:07.790 --> 00:53:12.790
I was really happy with the way my job was described

1075
00:53:13.240 --> 00:53:16.230
that I would be an ambassador to African women,

1076
00:53:16.230 --> 00:53:17.970
and I would help them set up

1077
00:53:17.970 --> 00:53:21.670
these little microfinance entities across the continent,

1078
00:53:21.670 --> 00:53:24.163
starting in the Ivory Coast, the Cote d'Ivoire.

1079
00:53:25.240 --> 00:53:28.163
I had an office at the African Development Bank.

1080
00:53:31.717 --> 00:53:35.153
And it all just sounded amazing to me,

1081
00:53:35.153 --> 00:53:38.023
like this amazing entrepreneurial opportunity.

1082
00:53:39.250 --> 00:53:42.680
Of course, I get there and I'm fully and wholly rejected

1083
00:53:42.680 --> 00:53:45.223
by the West African women.

1084
00:53:46.650 --> 00:53:50.880
Wondering why a 25-year old who spoke terrible French

1085
00:53:50.880 --> 00:53:53.510
and had no understanding of their culture

1086
00:53:54.520 --> 00:53:57.740
should be the ambassador to African women.

1087
00:53:57.740 --> 00:53:58.573
Good point.

1088
00:53:58.573 --> 00:53:59.590
The white savior

1089
00:53:59.590 --> 00:54:02.082
here to save the world.
White savior.

1090
00:54:02.082 --> 00:54:03.582
Eye roles ensue.

1091
00:54:04.870 --> 00:54:07.000
And no one wants saving, thank you very much.

1092
00:54:07.000 --> 00:54:12.000
And certainly not by people who haven't even spent any time,

1093
00:54:12.680 --> 00:54:17.680
truly even understanding who you are, why you are.

1094
00:54:19.760 --> 00:54:22.620
And second, this was a very prestigious office.

1095
00:54:22.620 --> 00:54:26.230
And so there was also the other side

1096
00:54:26.230 --> 00:54:28.223
that why should I get it, not them?

1097
00:54:31.416 --> 00:54:36.416
And so I so ensued probably six months of failure

1098
00:54:38.100 --> 00:54:39.233
after failure.

1099
00:54:41.000 --> 00:54:45.013
Really, really difficult situations that were,

1100
00:54:45.970 --> 00:54:49.010
many of them from my own blind spots

1101
00:54:49.010 --> 00:54:52.010
and some from very ambitious women

1102
00:54:52.010 --> 00:54:54.570
who saw me in the way of their ambition.

1103
00:54:54.570 --> 00:54:56.743
And that was a very toxic combination.

1104
00:54:57.830 --> 00:54:59.280
So how long were you there?

1105
00:55:00.430 --> 00:55:05.423
I was only there for about six months but

1106
00:55:09.966 --> 00:55:11.760
it was definitely a defining moment.

1107
00:55:11.760 --> 00:55:13.970
One that almost broke me.

1108
00:55:13.970 --> 00:55:17.250
You know, they say, what doesn't kill you

1109
00:55:17.250 --> 00:55:18.300
makes you stronger.

1110
00:55:18.300 --> 00:55:20.543
Sometimes it just doesn't kill you.

1111
00:55:22.990 --> 00:55:25.400
I definitely remember walking down the street one day

1112
00:55:25.400 --> 00:55:28.090
where these dogs were growling at me

1113
00:55:28.090 --> 00:55:30.090
and just being like, go ahead.

1114
00:55:30.090 --> 00:55:32.275
You know, everyone else hates me too.

1115
00:55:32.275 --> 00:55:33.640
Oh, I know.
I was very low.

1116
00:55:33.640 --> 00:55:35.590
But it does make you stronger though,

1117
00:55:35.590 --> 00:55:39.380
because it's a formative experience in which you realize,

1118
00:55:39.380 --> 00:55:42.100
like you've been disabused of everything

1119
00:55:42.100 --> 00:55:45.110
that you kind of thought about how to approach these things.

1120
00:55:45.110 --> 00:55:48.590
So you depart with your tail between your legs,

1121
00:55:48.590 --> 00:55:50.360
but you've had a valuable experience.

1122
00:55:50.360 --> 00:55:52.980
I mean, you're very frank about these failures

1123
00:55:52.980 --> 00:55:54.210
that you've had along your path,

1124
00:55:54.210 --> 00:55:56.830
but each one of them kind of informs the next step

1125
00:55:56.830 --> 00:56:01.420
and all is part of this, you know, journey

1126
00:56:01.420 --> 00:56:06.050
towards really understanding how to be the best,

1127
00:56:06.050 --> 00:56:09.403
you know, steward and servant to these solutions.

1128
00:56:12.240 --> 00:56:15.623
Sure, it does make you stronger, there's no doubt.

1129
00:56:19.160 --> 00:56:24.160
It definitely, in a crazy way, reinforced my commitment.

1130
00:56:24.580 --> 00:56:25.883
I was not leaving.

1131
00:56:28.451 --> 00:56:30.500
And my parents lived in Germany.

1132
00:56:30.500 --> 00:56:32.550
My dad was still in the army then,

1133
00:56:32.550 --> 00:56:37.550
and I went to Heidelberg for Christmas time.

1134
00:56:39.300 --> 00:56:41.103
I was jaundiced.

1135
00:56:43.827 --> 00:56:45.070
I couldn't have been skinnier.

1136
00:56:45.070 --> 00:56:48.363
I was just this like tiny, tiny thing.

1137
00:56:50.080 --> 00:56:52.210
I was probably running 16 miles a day

1138
00:56:52.210 --> 00:56:56.693
just to probably run away, I don't know.

1139
00:56:59.700 --> 00:57:01.950
And then my mother and I had the big blowout.

1140
00:57:02.870 --> 00:57:06.337
Mom, I'd been-
Ooh, tell me about this.

1141
00:57:09.260 --> 00:57:12.500
Whether I had been deliberately poisoned

1142
00:57:12.500 --> 00:57:17.500
or I had mistakenly gotten the worst food poisoning

1143
00:57:19.070 --> 00:57:20.760
a person could get.

1144
00:57:20.760 --> 00:57:25.740
I lie in a bathroom floor for eight days,

1145
00:57:25.740 --> 00:57:29.463
really fully dehydrated and really in trouble.

1146
00:57:32.559 --> 00:57:34.610
I mean, just to be clear, when you left,

1147
00:57:34.610 --> 00:57:36.230
it was under a threat.

1148
00:57:36.230 --> 00:57:37.870
You know, there was-
There was threat.

1149
00:57:37.870 --> 00:57:40.340
Yeah, there was threats and you had been forewarned

1150
00:57:40.340 --> 00:57:42.780
that you could possibly be poisoned.

1151
00:57:42.780 --> 00:57:43.613
Yeah.

1152
00:57:43.613 --> 00:57:45.940
And you ultimately do get sick and it's unclear

1153
00:57:45.940 --> 00:57:47.470
whether you were actually poisoned

1154
00:57:47.470 --> 00:57:48.935
or suffered some kind of food poisoning or something.

1155
00:57:48.935 --> 00:57:50.580
It was definitely a crescendoing by the minute.

1156
00:57:50.580 --> 00:57:52.680
Yeah, but that's terrifying.

1157
00:57:52.680 --> 00:57:53.624
It was terrifying.

1158
00:57:53.624 --> 00:57:54.974
I mean, I left the country.

1159
00:57:57.150 --> 00:57:59.240
When I finally got well enough,

1160
00:57:59.240 --> 00:58:01.030
you know, I bought a ticket that day.

1161
00:58:01.030 --> 00:58:03.020
I did go to the women

1162
00:58:03.020 --> 00:58:05.563
in a way that does not sound like a broken person.

1163
00:58:07.720 --> 00:58:11.087
I remember looking at them, they were so beautiful.

1164
00:58:11.087 --> 00:58:15.710
And I was so conscious of being such a little bedraggled,

1165
00:58:15.710 --> 00:58:17.000
skinny, white thing.

1166
00:58:17.000 --> 00:58:20.690
And they were like these gorgeous women in these turbans

1167
00:58:20.690 --> 00:58:24.350
and jewelry and flowing robes of yellow and purple.

1168
00:58:24.350 --> 00:58:26.403
I mean, yellow and orange and pale blue.

1169
00:58:28.572 --> 00:58:30.780
I'm like there in the room with them.

1170
00:58:30.780 --> 00:58:34.473
And somehow I had the courage then to say,

1171
00:58:35.790 --> 00:58:37.240
I know I made a lot of mistakes,

1172
00:58:37.240 --> 00:58:42.240
but I wouldn't treat any creature the way you treated me

1173
00:58:43.900 --> 00:58:45.453
and I'm totally alone.

1174
00:58:48.467 --> 00:58:52.830
And we need to do better if we're going to heal this worlds.

1175
00:58:52.830 --> 00:58:56.690
That somehow those words came out

1176
00:58:56.690 --> 00:59:01.690
and I wasn't hectoring.

1177
00:59:02.990 --> 00:59:04.770
I was just, it makes me cry.

1178
00:59:04.770 --> 00:59:07.132
You know, I was just saying truths.

1179
00:59:07.132 --> 00:59:12.132
And they couldn't really take it in.

1180
00:59:13.100 --> 00:59:17.603
You know, it was just that then pregnant quiet.

1181
00:59:21.449 --> 00:59:26.449
And then I left, I left my two boxes of my prize poetry

1182
00:59:26.500 --> 00:59:29.590
and whatever random few possessions that I owned

1183
00:59:31.210 --> 00:59:33.973
in the hotel, never to go back and get it.

1184
00:59:36.040 --> 00:59:41.040
And so you can imagine my mother seeing this transformed,

1185
00:59:41.170 --> 00:59:45.380
young woman show up and announce,

1186
00:59:45.380 --> 00:59:46.960
well, now I'm going back to East Africa.

1187
00:59:46.960 --> 00:59:49.700
I still don't really have a job, but don't worry, mom,

1188
00:59:49.700 --> 00:59:51.330
it's gonna be fine.

1189
00:59:51.330 --> 00:59:53.630
You quickly recharged your battery and said,

1190
00:59:53.630 --> 00:59:55.575
I'm gonna go to Kenya, it was Kenya was next, right?

1191
00:59:55.575 --> 00:59:57.537
Kenya, Kenya.

1192
01:00:01.470 --> 01:00:04.550
Yeah, I don't even think I experienced my battery

1193
01:00:04.550 --> 01:00:07.780
as not charged at some level.

1194
01:00:07.780 --> 01:00:11.580
It was just, I'm gonna go,

1195
01:00:11.580 --> 01:00:14.323
I'm gonna be home, I am definitely gonna eat a lot.

1196
01:00:16.210 --> 01:00:19.770
My boyfriend met me in Germany

1197
01:00:19.770 --> 01:00:24.580
and everyone just was just like, you can't go back.

1198
01:00:24.580 --> 01:00:27.573
And I felt like there was a form almost of,

1199
01:00:28.560 --> 01:00:30.730
that their fear couldn't hold me back

1200
01:00:30.730 --> 01:00:32.440
and that if I didn't go,

1201
01:00:32.440 --> 01:00:37.440
it was going to affirm too many people's expectations

1202
01:00:37.580 --> 01:00:42.580
of what this place was, this continent was, that I knew

1203
01:00:42.830 --> 01:00:45.297
that even though these particular individuals and I

1204
01:00:45.297 --> 01:00:47.500
had had a hard experience,

1205
01:00:47.500 --> 01:00:51.760
I already had seen how extraordinary people were

1206
01:00:51.760 --> 01:00:53.840
and what was possible.

1207
01:00:53.840 --> 01:00:56.690
I knew I was in love with it.

1208
01:00:56.690 --> 01:01:00.760
And so, again, going from duality to the complexity

1209
01:01:00.760 --> 01:01:03.023
of what it means to be human.

1210
01:01:04.690 --> 01:01:06.000
I wasn't lying to that.

1211
01:01:06.000 --> 01:01:11.000
In fact, I was so drawn to life there

1212
01:01:11.540 --> 01:01:14.063
and to the people there and to the culture there.

1213
01:01:15.090 --> 01:01:20.090
But just as I could not go in any longer as a savior,

1214
01:01:22.220 --> 01:01:27.220
nor could I any longer see Africa in inverted quotes

1215
01:01:27.330 --> 01:01:31.410
as a perfect place where a community flourished

1216
01:01:31.410 --> 01:01:33.223
and everyone was kind to each other.

1217
01:01:34.620 --> 01:01:39.620
I went back after those two weeks as a,

1218
01:01:41.290 --> 01:01:46.290
a much more nuanced person going to invest, if you will.

1219
01:01:50.790 --> 01:01:53.140
Was a clear understanding that this was about

1220
01:01:54.970 --> 01:01:57.280
the human journey that I was a part of.

1221
01:01:57.280 --> 01:01:59.350
I was neither in control of it,

1222
01:01:59.350 --> 01:02:01.580
nor would I be controlled by it,

1223
01:02:01.580 --> 01:02:05.210
but that I wanted to engage in relationship.

1224
01:02:05.210 --> 01:02:07.023
And so to your point,

1225
01:02:08.040 --> 01:02:10.090
what doesn't kill you makes you stronger,

1226
01:02:14.015 --> 01:02:18.897
it was a rapid rinse and forget the dry cycle,

1227
01:02:19.740 --> 01:02:21.880
just throw you right back

1228
01:02:21.880 --> 01:02:23.700
but as a completely different person.

1229
01:02:23.700 --> 01:02:26.650
Right, with kind of this hope,

1230
01:02:26.650 --> 01:02:30.680
but a more harder edged hope and an appreciation

1231
01:02:30.680 --> 01:02:34.540
for the ferocity that is gonna be demanded of you

1232
01:02:34.540 --> 01:02:37.970
in order to just survive and make your way,

1233
01:02:37.970 --> 01:02:40.610
let alone, you know, produce change

1234
01:02:42.270 --> 01:02:43.730
or catalyze change.

1235
01:02:43.730 --> 01:02:48.310
And the beginning may be of a superpower

1236
01:02:48.310 --> 01:02:51.840
that I think that you have too

1237
01:02:53.070 --> 01:02:58.070
is being able to sit in a room and just really pay attention

1238
01:02:59.770 --> 01:03:02.990
to the room and to the people in the room,

1239
01:03:02.990 --> 01:03:06.710
not in a naive, idealistic, nor cynical way,

1240
01:03:06.710 --> 01:03:10.690
but start to figure out who's who in the room,

1241
01:03:10.690 --> 01:03:13.290
what dynamics are happening in the room?

1242
01:03:13.290 --> 01:03:15.740
I think that the experience in Cote d'Ivoire

1243
01:03:17.020 --> 01:03:20.853
really jump started it and then accelerated that skillset.

1244
01:03:25.120 --> 01:03:30.120
So that I could get really tangible things done

1245
01:03:31.570 --> 01:03:34.453
without losing that sense of idealism and hope.

1246
01:03:35.550 --> 01:03:37.200
And how long were you in Kenya?

1247
01:03:38.300 --> 01:03:41.710
Well, it's complicated because I was only in Kenya

1248
01:03:41.710 --> 01:03:44.803
a few months where I had another flaming fail,

1249
01:03:46.740 --> 01:03:51.740
that when these Rwandan women walked into my office there

1250
01:03:52.590 --> 01:03:57.590
and said, we are from Rwanda, I thought they said Uganda.

1251
01:03:57.690 --> 01:04:01.300
That's how I was at the time.

1252
01:04:01.300 --> 01:04:05.100
And they just passed a new law,

1253
01:04:05.100 --> 01:04:07.510
changing the Napoleonic code 1986,

1254
01:04:07.510 --> 01:04:09.870
so that women no longer had to get

1255
01:04:09.870 --> 01:04:13.563
their husband's permission when they opened a bank count.

1256
01:04:15.287 --> 01:04:18.810
And they wanted to see whether it might be possible to have

1257
01:04:18.810 --> 01:04:22.030
some kind of financial institution for women.

1258
01:04:22.030 --> 01:04:24.370
And it was the first time that African women

1259
01:04:24.370 --> 01:04:25.750
had ever asked me to do anything.

1260
01:04:25.750 --> 01:04:29.950
And so I said, yes.

1261
01:04:29.950 --> 01:04:32.090
Yeah, you're being invited,

1262
01:04:32.090 --> 01:04:33.980
which is a big difference.
Invited in.

1263
01:04:33.980 --> 01:04:35.943
Come in.
It was Fulikila,

1264
01:04:35.943 --> 01:04:36.843
was that her name?

1265
01:04:38.690 --> 01:04:39.523
Or was she later the-

1266
01:04:39.523 --> 01:04:41.970
She was a woman named Landrata who I changed her name

1267
01:04:41.970 --> 01:04:45.120
in "The Blue Sweater" and Rabokata,

1268
01:04:45.120 --> 01:04:47.180
who I changed both of their names

1269
01:04:47.180 --> 01:04:49.380
'cause I was worried when I wrote that book.

1270
01:04:56.160 --> 01:04:59.410
I went sort of similar.

1271
01:04:59.410 --> 01:05:04.410
I went with one suitcase, and then for the next two years,

1272
01:05:06.900 --> 01:05:09.700
I pretty much went back and forth between Rwanda and Kenya.

1273
01:05:09.700 --> 01:05:11.770
I would've stayed in Rwanda,

1274
01:05:11.770 --> 01:05:15.163
but early on a woman named Immaculae,

1275
01:05:19.317 --> 01:05:20.580
who was one of our co-founders

1276
01:05:20.580 --> 01:05:22.863
and one of the first three parliamentarians.

1277
01:05:24.600 --> 01:05:29.600
I got malaria and she gave me a big gift and said,

1278
01:05:30.257 --> 01:05:31.630
"We love working with you, this is great.

1279
01:05:31.630 --> 01:05:34.220
We're building this bank, but it is

1280
01:05:35.930 --> 01:05:39.200
you are maniacal.

1281
01:05:39.200 --> 01:05:41.890
You know, you're just going so fast

1282
01:05:41.890 --> 01:05:45.340
and our lives don't work that way.

1283
01:05:45.340 --> 01:05:49.070
And so if we're truly going to build this

1284
01:05:49.070 --> 01:05:52.003
as a Rwandan institution, which I was committed to do,

1285
01:05:53.130 --> 01:05:54.110
we've gotta do it in a way

1286
01:05:54.110 --> 01:05:56.257
that doesn't fully depend on you."

1287
01:05:57.290 --> 01:06:01.650
And I was both crushed and exhilarated

1288
01:06:01.650 --> 01:06:04.120
because I did want this to be a Rwandan institution.

1289
01:06:04.120 --> 01:06:06.760
I didn't wanna stay there for the rest of my life.

1290
01:06:06.760 --> 01:06:09.900
And so we came up with this plan

1291
01:06:09.900 --> 01:06:14.390
that I would work there for two months

1292
01:06:14.390 --> 01:06:16.550
and then I would go to Kenya and work there for two months.

1293
01:06:16.550 --> 01:06:19.780
And there was another woman who was a dear

1294
01:06:19.780 --> 01:06:23.010
and is a dear mentor from the Philippines, Mary Rosales.

1295
01:06:23.010 --> 01:06:25.960
She ran UNICEF regionally

1296
01:06:25.960 --> 01:06:30.350
and she saw that we were building a bank for women.

1297
01:06:30.350 --> 01:06:35.350
And Mary, who was the one I would really go to cry with

1298
01:06:35.800 --> 01:06:37.200
when things got really hard,

1299
01:06:38.350 --> 01:06:41.370
she said, "We have a lot of work to do with women

1300
01:06:41.370 --> 01:06:44.800
and enterprise and income generation.

1301
01:06:44.800 --> 01:06:46.940
And so don't worry about it.

1302
01:06:46.940 --> 01:06:49.500
We have a job for you when you're in Kenya."

1303
01:06:49.500 --> 01:06:54.000
And so I would flip back and forth between the two countries

1304
01:06:54.000 --> 01:06:55.173
for the next two years.

1305
01:06:56.540 --> 01:07:00.760
And this bank ends up taking root and becoming successful.

1306
01:07:00.760 --> 01:07:04.350
And what's interesting is that,

1307
01:07:04.350 --> 01:07:06.450
I mean, first, you know, I wanna spend some time

1308
01:07:06.450 --> 01:07:11.450
focusing on the fact that so much of the work that you do

1309
01:07:14.140 --> 01:07:17.240
and truly the solutions that you're committed to

1310
01:07:17.240 --> 01:07:19.420
are oriented around women.

1311
01:07:19.420 --> 01:07:22.690
And in particular with microfinance,

1312
01:07:22.690 --> 01:07:24.190
it's all about the women, right?

1313
01:07:24.190 --> 01:07:27.240
The women being the kind of financial bookkeepers

1314
01:07:27.240 --> 01:07:30.330
and the decision makers about how funds are allocated

1315
01:07:30.330 --> 01:07:33.950
and the empowerment of these women to create

1316
01:07:33.950 --> 01:07:37.280
some level of financial independence

1317
01:07:38.280 --> 01:07:42.960
and how that really creates a foundation

1318
01:07:42.960 --> 01:07:47.960
for a more functional society.

1319
01:07:48.170 --> 01:07:49.540
So talk a little bit about

1320
01:07:49.540 --> 01:07:54.540
like the women being like the key component in all of this.

1321
01:07:56.640 --> 01:07:58.033
So I never say this.

1322
01:07:59.450 --> 01:08:04.450
But when I first went to work with women in banking

1323
01:08:08.100 --> 01:08:11.610
and women in Africa, it felt a little funny to be honest,

1324
01:08:11.610 --> 01:08:14.020
because I was raised, again, a different generation

1325
01:08:14.020 --> 01:08:17.430
with four alpha male brothers

1326
01:08:17.430 --> 01:08:21.780
in a big patriarchal extended family.

1327
01:08:21.780 --> 01:08:24.740
And so I spent a lot of my youth

1328
01:08:24.740 --> 01:08:27.710
proving that I was as tough as the boys were.

1329
01:08:27.710 --> 01:08:32.710
And so this idea that now I'm going to do women's things

1330
01:08:32.880 --> 01:08:34.730
was not comfortable at the beginning.

1331
01:08:35.950 --> 01:08:37.250
And then I moved to Africa

1332
01:08:38.150 --> 01:08:42.483
and saw that 80% of the food was grown by the women.

1333
01:08:45.720 --> 01:08:47.550
Especially in West Africa,

1334
01:08:47.550 --> 01:08:51.650
you actually divided who earned income and what was paid for

1335
01:08:51.650 --> 01:08:54.560
by whom because you had these polygamous families.

1336
01:08:54.560 --> 01:08:57.243
And my whole world shifted.

1337
01:08:59.220 --> 01:09:03.540
When I saw, firsthand, how women were excluded,

1338
01:09:03.540 --> 01:09:05.900
how women, yet women were the backbone.

1339
01:09:05.900 --> 01:09:07.180
They were the backbone of family.

1340
01:09:07.180 --> 01:09:09.940
They were the backbone of who paid for school,

1341
01:09:09.940 --> 01:09:12.430
who made sure the kids got educated.

1342
01:09:12.430 --> 01:09:15.740
They were the backbone for culture.

1343
01:09:15.740 --> 01:09:19.100
And they did so much of the work

1344
01:09:19.100 --> 01:09:24.100
and yet were seen as chattel, as possessions, had no rights,

1345
01:09:24.430 --> 01:09:25.453
were not schooled.

1346
01:09:29.712 --> 01:09:31.400
And that fundamentally changed me.

1347
01:09:31.400 --> 01:09:33.130
And in some ways,

1348
01:09:33.130 --> 01:09:38.130
it is often the ones that are the doubting but curious

1349
01:09:39.170 --> 01:09:40.610
that I think become

1350
01:09:43.190 --> 01:09:45.240
maybe the best ambassadors,

1351
01:09:45.240 --> 01:09:48.180
because I never used the language when would see women

1352
01:09:48.180 --> 01:09:49.950
because I couldn't stand the language

1353
01:09:49.950 --> 01:09:51.020
of women in development.

1354
01:09:51.020 --> 01:09:52.890
Women are more honest, women are more this,

1355
01:09:52.890 --> 01:09:53.790
and I would think,

1356
01:09:54.710 --> 01:09:55.823
people are people.

1357
01:09:56.660 --> 01:10:01.660
However, what I did see was how women were systematically,

1358
01:10:03.010 --> 01:10:06.810
fundamentally excluded from opportunities

1359
01:10:06.810 --> 01:10:10.120
that would actually enable economy to flourish.

1360
01:10:10.120 --> 01:10:12.550
And that we will never build a world

1361
01:10:12.550 --> 01:10:17.540
when we systematically oppress and in some cases erase

1362
01:10:17.540 --> 01:10:19.003
50% of the population.

1363
01:10:20.800 --> 01:10:25.800
So that changed, again, a new layer of nuance,

1364
01:10:26.750 --> 01:10:29.750
that if there would be one man in the room at the time,

1365
01:10:29.750 --> 01:10:31.250
women wouldn't tell the truth.

1366
01:10:32.490 --> 01:10:34.670
And so I had to make rules.

1367
01:10:34.670 --> 01:10:36.090
If we were talking about Duterimbere,

1368
01:10:36.090 --> 01:10:37.530
which was the name of our bank,

1369
01:10:37.530 --> 01:10:39.660
the men weren't allowed.

1370
01:10:39.660 --> 01:10:41.270
Women needed to have that place

1371
01:10:41.270 --> 01:10:42.550
where they had the confidence

1372
01:10:42.550 --> 01:10:47.550
and the freedom to tell the truth.

1373
01:10:49.640 --> 01:10:51.010
Sorry to interrupt the flow.

1374
01:10:51.010 --> 01:10:52.830
We'll be right back with more awesome.

1375
01:10:52.830 --> 01:10:55.370
But first I do wanna snag a quick moment

1376
01:10:55.370 --> 01:10:57.300
to talk about something I care a lot about,

1377
01:10:57.300 --> 01:10:59.530
which is the importance of nutrition.

1378
01:10:59.530 --> 01:11:00.870
And the thing is,

1379
01:11:00.870 --> 01:11:04.070
most people I know actually aspire to eat better,

1380
01:11:04.070 --> 01:11:07.450
to incorporate more whole plants, fruits, vegetables, seeds,

1381
01:11:07.450 --> 01:11:10.550
beans and legumes into their daily regimen.

1382
