All right, back to the pod.

1405
01:12:17.870 --> 01:12:20.380
There was this idea, you know,

1406
01:12:20.380 --> 01:12:22.150
in the early days of microfinance

1407
01:12:22.150 --> 01:12:24.700
that it would just be throwing money away,

1408
01:12:24.700 --> 01:12:26.790
that these people would not repay their loans.

1409
01:12:26.790 --> 01:12:28.510
And in fact, it's quite the opposite.

1410
01:12:28.510 --> 01:12:32.600
Like the repayment percentage is way higher than it is

1411
01:12:32.600 --> 01:12:36.020
for loans doled out to, you know, higher-

1412
01:12:36.020 --> 01:12:38.033
People like us.
Socio-economic echelons.

1413
01:12:39.170 --> 01:12:41.120
But that was also a lesson that you learned, right?

1414
01:12:41.120 --> 01:12:43.630
Because initially there were some issues around that

1415
01:12:43.630 --> 01:12:47.840
until you built in the accountability piece

1416
01:12:47.840 --> 01:12:51.980
that has the added bonus effect of also kind of

1417
01:12:51.980 --> 01:12:53.810
sewing the seeds of dignity,

1418
01:12:53.810 --> 01:12:56.733
which is like a huge part of this whole mission.

1419
01:12:57.730 --> 01:12:58.563
Yeah.

1420
01:12:59.910 --> 01:13:02.960
Rwanda was a big age recipient.

1421
01:13:02.960 --> 01:13:06.770
And so the way people interacted with

1422
01:13:06.770 --> 01:13:11.770
particularly anything foreign was to accept grants.

1423
01:13:11.990 --> 01:13:13.521
You'd walk down the street and kids would say,

1424
01:13:13.521 --> 01:13:15.213
(speaks in foreign language)

1425
01:13:15.213 --> 01:13:16.820
Give me 10 franks.

1426
01:13:16.820 --> 01:13:19.230
And I would say, no, no, no, no, no.

1427
01:13:19.230 --> 01:13:20.063
I'd be like,

1428
01:13:20.063 --> 01:13:20.896
(speaks in foreign language)
10 franks.

1429
01:13:20.896 --> 01:13:22.220
No, you give it to me.

1430
01:13:22.220 --> 01:13:25.270
And they would look at me like, but you're rich madam.

1431
01:13:25.270 --> 01:13:29.630
And I'd say, let's talk, let's have a conversation.

1432
01:13:29.630 --> 01:13:33.970
It so offended me that we had these so many people

1433
01:13:33.970 --> 01:13:37.920
who were interacting in a way that was tantamount to begging

1434
01:13:37.920 --> 01:13:41.510
and not enabling themselves to be who they could be.

1435
01:13:41.510 --> 01:13:43.090
So when we first started,

1436
01:13:43.090 --> 01:13:45.730
particularly this bakery that I started,

1437
01:13:45.730 --> 01:13:47.513
the women ripped us off blindly.

1438
01:13:48.470 --> 01:13:49.303
It didn't take much.
Because it was

1439
01:13:49.303 --> 01:13:50.370
government run, right?

1440
01:13:50.370 --> 01:13:52.170
It was like this charitable organization.

1441
01:13:52.170 --> 01:13:53.003
So nobody was invested.
It was run

1442
01:13:53.003 --> 01:13:55.050
by the good sisters.

1443
01:13:55.050 --> 01:13:57.170
It was a charitable organization, not government,

1444
01:13:57.170 --> 01:13:58.930
but they

1445
01:14:01.090 --> 01:14:03.400
probably wisely took what they could take

1446
01:14:04.780 --> 01:14:06.820
when it was clear.

1447
01:14:06.820 --> 01:14:09.550
And they finally believed me that this wasn't mine,

1448
01:14:09.550 --> 01:14:11.160
this was theirs.

1449
01:14:11.160 --> 01:14:13.193
And so we had a choice.

1450
01:14:14.340 --> 01:14:17.810
We build it in a way that is financially sustainable

1451
01:14:17.810 --> 01:14:20.673
and then allows them to grow and prosper.

1452
01:14:22.010 --> 01:14:24.570
Or it becomes like too many of the other stupid projects

1453
01:14:24.570 --> 01:14:26.320
that we had seen where it works for a few years

1454
01:14:26.320 --> 01:14:28.100
and then it's gone.

1455
01:14:28.100 --> 01:14:29.090
How does that happen?

1456
01:14:29.090 --> 01:14:32.040
There are so many well-intentioned people in philanthropy,

1457
01:14:32.040 --> 01:14:36.380
and yet, you know, there's just too many stories

1458
01:14:36.380 --> 01:14:39.530
of, you know, funds just ending up-

1459
01:14:39.530 --> 01:14:41.030
You know, there's all kinds of

1460
01:14:44.060 --> 01:14:45.350
issues around corruption

1461
01:14:45.350 --> 01:14:47.940
and just organizations being poorly run.

1462
01:14:47.940 --> 01:14:49.380
And the money seems to disappear

1463
01:14:49.380 --> 01:14:51.510
and nobody ends up benefiting and it's all like

1464
01:14:51.510 --> 01:14:53.193
just a big cluster folk.

1465
01:14:54.440 --> 01:14:58.510
Like how does that happen with people who I'm sure

1466
01:14:58.510 --> 01:15:01.093
are trying to do the right thing and help?

1467
01:15:04.720 --> 01:15:07.893
I often say that distance dulls the moral imagination.

1468
01:15:08.750 --> 01:15:11.350
And so we wanna do good.

1469
01:15:11.350 --> 01:15:13.640
We wanna be part of good.

1470
01:15:13.640 --> 01:15:18.530
And so we give money hoping it does good,

1471
01:15:18.530 --> 01:15:20.210
but that's not enough.

1472
01:15:20.210 --> 01:15:25.210
We've actually got to build in systems that have like,

1473
01:15:25.260 --> 01:15:26.760
to your word, of accountability,

1474
01:15:26.760 --> 01:15:28.710
generosity without accountability

1475
01:15:30.430 --> 01:15:32.730
can really make a mess of things.

1476
01:15:32.730 --> 01:15:34.660
And it's not the same as justice.

1477
01:15:34.660 --> 01:15:36.053
Justice is hard.

1478
01:15:37.240 --> 01:15:40.370
Generosity can be hard, but it's a lot easier than justice.

1479
01:15:40.370 --> 01:15:45.370
And so when there's no accountability and systems,

1480
01:15:45.520 --> 01:15:47.300
and if you think about an aid system

1481
01:15:47.300 --> 01:15:49.710
where people are coming and going,

1482
01:15:49.710 --> 01:15:53.160
they're not vested, it becomes even easy.

1483
01:15:53.160 --> 01:15:57.780
Or for many of the different flares to take different pieces

1484
01:15:57.780 --> 01:15:59.330
of whatever is available.

1485
01:15:59.330 --> 01:16:01.570
So it's gotten a lot better,

1486
01:16:01.570 --> 01:16:06.570
but I did this study of 200 of these women's groups

1487
01:16:06.720 --> 01:16:10.490
in Kenya in one of the two months that I was there.

1488
01:16:10.490 --> 01:16:15.490
And what I saw was a pretty terrifying cycle

1489
01:16:15.530 --> 01:16:18.810
where the philanthropist or the aid organization

1490
01:16:18.810 --> 01:16:22.050
would give money to a women's group.

1491
01:16:22.050 --> 01:16:24.710
They immediately would give a 10% kickback

1492
01:16:24.710 --> 01:16:27.860
to the local district officer

1493
01:16:27.860 --> 01:16:30.100
because the money had to go through him.

1494
01:16:30.100 --> 01:16:32.530
Then they had no skills

1495
01:16:32.530 --> 01:16:36.530
to cooperatively run their chicken farm

1496
01:16:36.530 --> 01:16:38.220
or whatever they were given the money for,

1497
01:16:38.220 --> 01:16:42.653
usually decided by some government official or a foreigner.

1498
01:16:43.740 --> 01:16:45.050
The whole thing would fail.

1499
01:16:45.050 --> 01:16:48.600
But whenever the dignitaries would come

1500
01:16:48.600 --> 01:16:50.710
to see how the project was doing,

1501
01:16:50.710 --> 01:16:53.480
the women would parade out some little chicks,

1502
01:16:53.480 --> 01:16:56.937
they'd put a show on, they would go by Fantas and biscuits.

1503
01:16:59.070 --> 01:17:01.040
Sometimes they would kill a goat.

1504
01:17:01.040 --> 01:17:04.410
And after looking at these 200 groups,

1505
01:17:04.410 --> 01:17:06.930
I could only conclude that the majority of them

1506
01:17:06.930 --> 01:17:11.930
were spending money to keep this whole farce alive.

1507
01:17:12.010 --> 01:17:15.060
And I remember staying up all night, one night,

1508
01:17:15.060 --> 01:17:19.440
writing the report and the first line was,

1509
01:17:19.440 --> 01:17:21.230
good intentions lead the path to hell,

1510
01:17:21.230 --> 01:17:23.423
another famous saying of my mother.

1511
01:17:25.040 --> 01:17:29.690
And I just felt such rage that there was a machine

1512
01:17:29.690 --> 01:17:33.110
that ultimately was a big lie

1513
01:17:34.100 --> 01:17:37.370
that if we really cared about enabling people

1514
01:17:37.370 --> 01:17:39.480
to solve problems,

1515
01:17:39.480 --> 01:17:41.960
this is not the machine we would be doing.

1516
01:17:41.960 --> 01:17:45.090
And on top of that,

1517
01:17:45.090 --> 01:17:48.440
you know, as generous as the spirit is that's donating

1518
01:17:48.440 --> 01:17:51.970
all of these things, it doesn't respect the dignity piece

1519
01:17:51.970 --> 01:17:55.980
because nobody wants to be just a charity case, right?

1520
01:17:55.980 --> 01:17:59.443
Like this is another kind of facet of the things

1521
01:17:59.443 --> 01:18:01.600
that you talk about out in the book,

1522
01:18:01.600 --> 01:18:05.060
which is finding a way to inspire that dignity,

1523
01:18:05.060 --> 01:18:09.210
and that comes with this model and this approach

1524
01:18:09.210 --> 01:18:13.990
where these people aren't on the receiving end of charity,

1525
01:18:13.990 --> 01:18:16.050
they become stakeholders and invested.

1526
01:18:16.050 --> 01:18:17.890
So you're aligning the incentives, right?

1527
01:18:17.890 --> 01:18:19.660
A lot of these problems you're talking about,

1528
01:18:19.660 --> 01:18:22.150
there's a misalignment of incentives

1529
01:18:22.150 --> 01:18:24.620
that butts up against a lack of accountability

1530
01:18:24.620 --> 01:18:27.210
that creates like all of these problems.

1531
01:18:27.210 --> 01:18:30.740
And Acumen is really a re-imagination of the model

1532
01:18:30.740 --> 01:18:34.050
that is this hybrid between philanthropy

1533
01:18:34.050 --> 01:18:38.960
and, you know, proper investment banking or venture capital

1534
01:18:38.960 --> 01:18:43.960
to get those incentives in parallel with the best interest

1535
01:18:44.840 --> 01:18:45.980
of solving the problem

1536
01:18:45.980 --> 01:18:48.540
and getting people really engaged themselves,

1537
01:18:48.540 --> 01:18:51.100
the people who are on the receiving end of it.

1538
01:18:51.100 --> 01:18:52.445
Is that fair?

1539
01:18:52.445 --> 01:18:53.445
(indistinct)

1540
01:18:53.445 --> 01:18:55.470
I don't know, I don't feel like I articulated

1541
01:18:55.470 --> 01:18:56.303
that very well at all.

1542
01:18:56.303 --> 01:18:57.543
It was beautiful.

1543
01:18:58.460 --> 01:19:01.490
You articulated it beautifully because yeah,

1544
01:19:01.490 --> 01:19:03.230
what I learned more than anything else

1545
01:19:03.230 --> 01:19:05.230
and certainly after the one in genocide,

1546
01:19:07.020 --> 01:19:10.840
but in story after story like this is that

1547
01:19:11.710 --> 01:19:16.620
many well-intended people see a chance to give a grant

1548
01:19:16.620 --> 01:19:18.530
and get people a little bit more income

1549
01:19:18.530 --> 01:19:20.330
for their income-generating project.

1550
01:19:21.558 --> 01:19:26.320
But that income and wealth is not the opposite of poverty,

1551
01:19:26.320 --> 01:19:28.440
that the opposite of poverty truly is dignity.

1552
01:19:28.440 --> 01:19:31.090
It's that ability to make choice.

1553
01:19:31.090 --> 01:19:35.703
And so when I started Acumen,

1554
01:19:36.880 --> 01:19:40.660
the whole focus was on what systems would you build

1555
01:19:40.660 --> 01:19:42.990
to enable human dignity

1556
01:19:42.990 --> 01:19:45.490
rather than just make sure people get some income.

1557
01:19:47.690 --> 01:19:49.290
And it felt very clear to me then,

1558
01:19:49.290 --> 01:19:52.210
particularly after all of these experiences,

1559
01:19:52.210 --> 01:19:55.680
that markets have a real role to play

1560
01:19:55.680 --> 01:20:00.680
because there's a distribution and a scaling that's natural.

1561
01:20:01.610 --> 01:20:03.500
You have self-organizing mechanisms

1562
01:20:03.500 --> 01:20:06.530
through the good part of capitalism

1563
01:20:06.530 --> 01:20:08.080
and it's limited.

1564
01:20:08.080 --> 01:20:11.480
It too often leaves out the poor and in the worst of cases,

1565
01:20:11.480 --> 01:20:13.330
it exploits the poor.

1566
01:20:13.330 --> 01:20:18.330
Charity has a role, but it too often creates dependency.

1567
01:20:19.460 --> 01:20:24.460
So what if we took philanthropy and invested it

1568
01:20:24.540 --> 01:20:26.740
in those entrepreneurs that were hell-bent

1569
01:20:26.740 --> 01:20:28.640
on solving some of the biggest problems of our day,

1570
01:20:28.640 --> 01:20:31.360
like sanitation, like education, like energy,

1571
01:20:31.360 --> 01:20:32.483
like agriculture?

1572
01:20:33.380 --> 01:20:37.620
What if we gave them time to really understand

1573
01:20:37.620 --> 01:20:39.860
the true constraints and the obstacles

1574
01:20:39.860 --> 01:20:44.340
that get in poor people's way and build solutions

1575
01:20:44.340 --> 01:20:46.680
so that at the end of the day,

1576
01:20:46.680 --> 01:20:49.330
they could send their children to schools

1577
01:20:49.330 --> 01:20:50.890
they wanted to send their children to,

1578
01:20:50.890 --> 01:20:53.760
they could get access to good healthcare,

1579
01:20:53.760 --> 01:20:58.265
they could get electricity.

1580
01:20:58.265 --> 01:21:01.210
Although I wasn't thinking about electricity 20 years ago.

1581
01:21:01.210 --> 01:21:05.730
And because what I'd seen over and over

1582
01:21:05.730 --> 01:21:09.710
was that the poor actually pay more in these broken markets

1583
01:21:09.710 --> 01:21:12.190
than the middle class pay anyway.

1584
01:21:12.190 --> 01:21:13.800
It's not like the poor is sitting around

1585
01:21:13.800 --> 01:21:15.980
waiting for somebody to get them water.

1586
01:21:15.980 --> 01:21:17.460
It just happens to be dirty.

1587
01:21:17.460 --> 01:21:20.640
And the water they get access to is often supplied by,

1588
01:21:20.640 --> 01:21:24.713
what's called mafias, extortionary providers.

1589
01:21:26.930 --> 01:21:31.930
And so it seemed to me that we could start with recognizing

1590
01:21:34.890 --> 01:21:38.420
that every human being wants to solve their own problems.

1591
01:21:38.420 --> 01:21:42.700
We wanna be part of contributing in one way or another,

1592
01:21:42.700 --> 01:21:46.710
and build those systems that, at the end of the day,

1593
01:21:46.710 --> 01:21:49.210
not only allow that kind of flourishing,

1594
01:21:49.210 --> 01:21:53.693
but solve all of our problems.

1595
01:21:55.320 --> 01:21:56.270
Well, first of all,

1596
01:21:56.270 --> 01:21:59.050
I think you should run your own marketing department

1597
01:21:59.050 --> 01:22:02.326
at your company because that was beautifully put.

1598
01:22:02.326 --> 01:22:03.610
Thank you.

1599
01:22:03.610 --> 01:22:07.405
And secondly, you know, this begs the question

1600
01:22:07.405 --> 01:22:12.405
of two things, first, this idea of patient capital,

1601
01:22:12.810 --> 01:22:15.720
because these, you know, if you're gonna change systems

1602
01:22:15.720 --> 01:22:18.410
and, you know, address infrastructure

1603
01:22:18.410 --> 01:22:21.530
and create these proper incentives and all of this,

1604
01:22:21.530 --> 01:22:22.397
like this is a lot of work

1605
01:22:22.397 --> 01:22:26.380
and this is gonna take a lot of time, complex, right.

1606
01:22:26.380 --> 01:22:28.733
And then second to that,

1607
01:22:30.850 --> 01:22:32.300
but very important of course,

1608
01:22:32.300 --> 01:22:37.260
is redefining how you think about success,

1609
01:22:37.260 --> 01:22:40.870
not in terms of profit and loss or investor return,

1610
01:22:40.870 --> 01:22:43.253
but more broadly in terms of,

1611
01:22:44.400 --> 01:22:46.700
I feel like the word impact gets thrown around a lot,

1612
01:22:46.700 --> 01:22:49.170
but like you tell me like what the metrics are

1613
01:22:49.170 --> 01:22:52.650
that you came up with in order to gauge whether,

1614
01:22:52.650 --> 01:22:55.730
you know, an infusion of capital or, you know,

1615
01:22:55.730 --> 01:22:58.440
lifting up entrepreneurs who are gonna go into these areas

1616
01:22:58.440 --> 01:23:00.040
and solve a particular problem,

1617
01:23:00.040 --> 01:23:03.780
like how you're you gauging their progress

1618
01:23:03.780 --> 01:23:07.733
and, you know, whether they're successful or not.

1619
01:23:09.700 --> 01:23:10.700
There are a lot of questions in that.

1620
01:23:10.700 --> 01:23:11.706
I know I was sorry,

1621
01:23:11.706 --> 01:23:12.539
that was more like a grunt.
No, that's good.

1622
01:23:12.539 --> 01:23:13.372
It's good, it's good, it's good.

1623
01:23:13.372 --> 01:23:14.205
I think I know exact,

1624
01:23:14.205 --> 01:23:15.112
I think I know where you're going-

1625
01:23:15.112 --> 01:23:16.540
I'm not very good at asking questions.

1626
01:23:16.540 --> 01:23:18.150
I just throw stuff out there.

1627
01:23:18.150 --> 01:23:20.000
You're awesome at asking questions.

1628
01:23:23.140 --> 01:23:27.010
And like I said, the honor of being listened to is just,

1629
01:23:27.010 --> 01:23:28.483
you're amazing, thank you.

1630
01:23:36.500 --> 01:23:38.390
Entrepreneurs that are building markets

1631
01:23:38.390 --> 01:23:42.163
that have never existed, don't know how to price.

1632
01:23:44.688 --> 01:23:47.401
They don't have a distribution center, system.

1633
01:23:47.401 --> 01:23:49.780
You know, you think about it, let's look at electricity,

1634
01:23:49.780 --> 01:23:52.480
which is one I've told before, but in 2006,

1635
01:23:53.909 --> 01:23:55.500
1.5 billion people on the planet

1636
01:23:55.500 --> 01:23:57.293
had no access to electricity.

1637
01:23:58.880 --> 01:24:01.180
Two young guys, Sam Goldman and Ned Tozun,

1638
01:24:01.180 --> 01:24:04.170
come forth with a solar lamp

1639
01:24:04.170 --> 01:24:05.700
which makes sense to people like us.

1640
01:24:05.700 --> 01:24:07.710
We've seen solar before.

1641
01:24:07.710 --> 01:24:09.900
What they didn't fully get

1642
01:24:09.900 --> 01:24:13.430
was that their customers would make two or $3 a day.

1643
01:24:13.430 --> 01:24:14.610
They would exist in places

1644
01:24:14.610 --> 01:24:16.570
that had very little infrastructure,

1645
01:24:16.570 --> 01:24:18.660
no distribution channels,

1646
01:24:18.660 --> 01:24:19.493
low income people

1647
01:24:19.493 --> 01:24:22.983
had no way to finance these solar lanterns.

1648
01:24:24.430 --> 01:24:26.910
And equally, if not more importantly,

1649
01:24:26.910 --> 01:24:30.380
they had very little trust of this new fangled technology

1650
01:24:30.380 --> 01:24:33.127
that may or may not work.

1651
01:24:33.127 --> 01:24:36.000
$30 was the first price point.

1652
01:24:36.000 --> 01:24:37.490
And that's a lot of money

1653
01:24:37.490 --> 01:24:40.080
for people who make two or $3 a day.

1654
01:24:40.080 --> 01:24:44.820
And so our idea was we would take this philanthropy.

1655
01:24:44.820 --> 01:24:46.083
We would invest,

1656
01:24:47.190 --> 01:24:48.830
I thought eight years would be long.

1657
01:24:48.830 --> 01:24:50.683
We're still in 14 years later.

1658
01:24:52.560 --> 01:24:56.480
And that we would accompany this, the company as well.

1659
01:24:56.480 --> 01:25:00.130
By that, I mean we would use our social capital,

1660
01:25:00.130 --> 01:25:02.830
our networks, our access to corporations,

1661
01:25:02.830 --> 01:25:04.580
we would help them raise grant money

1662
01:25:04.580 --> 01:25:06.180
so that they could build marketing systems

1663
01:25:06.180 --> 01:25:08.930
so that they could experiment, so that they could fail.

1664
01:25:10.625 --> 01:25:12.500
And any money that came back to Acumen

1665
01:25:12.500 --> 01:25:15.160
would be reinvested in other innovation

1666
01:25:15.160 --> 01:25:16.460
serving low income people.

1667
01:25:17.400 --> 01:25:21.980
That single company, d.light, has now brought

1668
01:25:21.980 --> 01:25:24.200
affordable light and electricity

1669
01:25:24.200 --> 01:25:27.740
to over a hundred million people.

1670
01:25:27.740 --> 01:25:31.470
We then continued to learn with them

1671
01:25:31.470 --> 01:25:34.530
and realized that we had to build a market.

1672
01:25:34.530 --> 01:25:38.560
And so we then started investing in other companies

1673
01:25:38.560 --> 01:25:42.240
like d.light as well as financing companies

1674
01:25:42.240 --> 01:25:46.260
and in mini grids and what was called nano grids,

1675
01:25:46.260 --> 01:25:48.513
like just a few houses hooked together.

1676
01:25:51.704 --> 01:25:55.870
And you asked, how did we know if we're making a difference?

1677
01:25:55.870 --> 01:25:57.840
How do you measure impact?

1678
01:25:57.840 --> 01:26:00.460
Well, we knew we could tell you how many people

1679
01:26:00.460 --> 01:26:02.310
